export const debate = {"id":"harris-dennett-free-will-2018","number":"146","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"Sam Harris vs Daniel Dennett: Does Free Will Exist?","label":"Determinism, agency, and responsibility","date":"2026-08-15","duration":"1 hr 35 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_J_9DKIAn48","motion":"Does determinism or indeterminism undermine free will and moral responsibility, or can reasons-responsive agency provide a compatibilist form of freedom worth preserving?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Harris challenges ultimate authorship under causation, while Dennett defends graded, reasons-responsive control and pragmatic responsibility without metaphysical self-creation.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"People feel like they are the authors of their thoughts","context":"Harris uses ordinary first-person experience to identify the authorship claim he believes compatibilist capacities leave unresolved."},"con":{"text":"the capacity to listen to reasons","context":"Dennett includes responsiveness to reasons among the graded competencies that distinguish responsible agency from impairment."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"No libertarian free will","speaker":"Sam Harris","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Compatibilist free will","speaker":"Daniel Dennett","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":82,"con":88},"sections":[{"sectionId":"free-will-concept","title":"The Concept and Stakes of Free Will","timebox":"5:02–39:55","score":{"pro":87,"con":83},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-free-will-first-person-authorship","time":"19:14","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Compatibilism preserves useful capacities but omits the first-person authorship and same-circumstances alternatives that motivate the original free-will problem.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply sharply distinguishes first-person authorship from third-person competence, identifying a genuine conceptual-preservation problem and directly confronting both compatibilist responsibility and naturalistic revision at their foundations. Principal limitation: Its generalized phenomenological description lacks representative support, and it leaves Dennett's detailed capacity package substantially unanswered while treating ordinary authorship intuitions as comparatively uniform across persons and contexts. Live burden: Harris must show that first-person origination and same-circumstances alternatives are indispensable criteria rather than revisable intuitions, while addressing why reasons-responsive competence cannot preserve the practically important phenomenon. Locked score: The strong band reflects coherent framing, meaningful burden contact, appropriate qualification, and a fair residual challenge, moderated by partial responsiveness, broad experiential scope, and insufficient engagement with the constructive competence account.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-fwc-compatible-responsibility","time":"5:02","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"The freedom important for moral responsibility can coexist with determinism, whereas libertarian or indeterminist freedom is an unnecessary philosophical fantasy.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The opening establishes an intelligible motion-level criterion by separating responsibility-supporting freedom from indeterministic exemption, thereby giving the compatibilist position immediate conceptual direction and argumentative relevance. Principal limitation: Its categorical dismissal of libertarian freedom receives comparatively abbreviated justification, while the crucial proposition that responsibility should govern conceptual preservation remains substantially implicit rather than independently demonstrated. Live burden: The account must explain why reasons-responsive capacities preserve enough of ordinary authorship and alternative possibility to constitute free will, rather than merely replacing the disputed phenomenon with socially valuable competence. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional burden contact, coherent conceptual organization, and charitable differentiation, moderated by limited evidentiary development and an insufficiently qualified declaration about libertarianism.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-libertarian-incoherence-redefinition","time":"38:57","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Libertarian freedom fits neither determinism nor indeterminism, while lawful graded capacities are a compatibilist redefinition rather than a solution to that dilemma.","score":92,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply formulates the determinism-indeterminism dilemma with exceptional clarity, concedes the reality of lawful graded capacities, and directly argues that compatibilism redescribes rather than resolves the original authorship problem. Principal limitation: Its exhaustiveness establishes difficulty for libertarian origination but does not independently prove that the libertarian conception controls legitimate free-will usage or that revisionary functional continuity is conceptually impermissible. Live burden: Harris must defend the relevance of the rejected conception after showing its incoherence and explain why preserving deliberation, self-control, and responsibility cannot justify a naturalistic reconstruction of freedom. Locked score: The very strong band reflects comprehensive target contact, stable terminology, disciplined concessions, and unusually coherent conceptual pressure, with only the contested criterion of conceptual ownership preventing the argument from conclusively settling the exchange.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-fwc-naturalistic-revision","time":"11:25","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Compatibilism can naturalize free will by discarding inherited magical commitments while preserving the functional phenomenon those commitments imperfectly describe.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The naturalistic-revision proposal supplies a coherent methodological rationale for retaining free-will language while removing supernatural commitments, and it directly advances the compatibilist preservation route at motion level. Principal limitation: The excerpt only sketches what the supposedly real phenomenon contains, so its contrast between functional continuity and inherited magic remains terminologically underdeveloped and somewhat more confident than demonstrated. Live burden: The proposal must specify which experiential, deliberative, and responsibility-related functions survive revision and explain why losing first-person origination and exact-replay alternatives does not amount to changing the subject. Locked score: The strong band recognizes direct relevance, recoverable structure, and a plausible naturalistic orientation, while registering limited warrant, partial term instability, and comparatively uncharitable treatment of the opposing conceptual concern.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"reasons-responsive-control","title":"Reasons-Responsive Competence and Bounded Control","timebox":"16:38–55:59","score":{"pro":85,"con":90},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-capacity-without-self-authorship","time":"44:17","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Reasons-responsive capacities differ genuinely, but agents never authored the genes, environments, conversations, and prior brain states determining those capacities and responses.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply grants genuine differences in voluntary control and responsiveness before tracing their formation to unchosen genes, environments, conversations, and prior neural conditions, thereby confronting compatibilism charitably. Principal limitation: Its compressed suggestion that persuasive responses are never picked underdescribes reflective attention, feedback, and temporally extended self-modification, which compatibilists can interpret as agent-level causal participation rather than ultimate self-creation. Live burden: Harris must establish why lack of authorship over formative inputs defeats bounded authorship over subsequent deliberation, instead of merely limiting the depth and independence of personal control. Locked score: The very strong band reflects precise concession, comprehensive causal analysis, strong responsiveness, and central burden contact, with a modest reduction for compressing recursive self-governance and assuming that ultimate origination is responsibility's necessary standard.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-rrc-graded-agency-package","time":"16:38","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Responsible agency comprises graded capacities for ordered desire, flexible attention, changing one's mind, responsiveness to reasons, and self-control rather than causal exemption.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move provides the debate's clearest constructive inventory of responsible agency, explicitly integrating ordered desires, attentional flexibility, reason-responsiveness, and self-control within a calibrated framework of graduated competence. Principal limitation: Although the capacities are precisely enumerated, the passage does not yet establish why possessing them supplies morally significant authorship rather than sophisticated behavioral organization implemented through unauthored causal conditions. Live burden: The competence package must connect these observable differences to genuine control while explaining how developmental luck, neural contingency, and unconscious implementation affect responsibility without collapsing every distinction. Locked score: The very strong band reflects outstanding clarity, careful qualification, matched argumentative force, and comprehensive central-burden support, with only the ultimate bridge from competence to responsibility remaining incompletely defended.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-competence-anomalous-lapse-tension","time":"53:04","role":"Diagnostic challenge","words":"A highly competent agent's rare failure may attract maximal blame while representing that agent least, suggesting causal luck rather than stable competence explains the anomaly.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The golfer analogy exposes an important tension between stable competence and anomalous performance, showing that maximum general ability can coexist with a failure that is exceptionally unrepresentative yet blame-attracting. Principal limitation: Missing a putt supplies only an analogy for moral culpability, and the hypothetical cosmic influence does not establish that anomalous wrongdoing is disconnected from reasons, precautions, or attributable character. Live burden: Harris must translate representativeness into a principled responsibility criterion and show why rare lapses undermine competence-based attribution rather than inviting finer analysis of control, negligence, and situational impairment. Locked score: The strong band reflects an inventive, responsive, cautiously framed diagnostic with clear conceptual pressure, moderated by analogical distance, speculative causation, and an incomplete account of how statistical abnormality bears upon moral responsibility.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-rrc-bounded-control","time":"47:53","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Control need not encompass every background condition; genuine agency consists in competent response to circumstances and cultivated, developmentally bounded self-governance.","score":90,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly replaces impossible total causal command with competent navigation of given conditions, while integrating moral education, cultivated self-control, developmental limitation, and degrees of diminished responsibility. Principal limitation: The account explains how bounded control operates but leaves partly unsettled why a capacity entirely shaped by unchosen antecedents should generate authorship rather than only prediction-sensitive behavioral competence. Live burden: Dennett must articulate the threshold and mechanism by which responsive self-maintenance becomes attributable agency, especially where luck affects capacity formation or produces isolated anomalous failures. Locked score: The very strong band reflects comprehensive responsiveness, stable definitions, explicit qualification, strong calibration, and a developed alternative to absolute responsibility, with only the deeper constitutive-luck bridge requiring additional philosophical defense.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"authorship-luck-neural-causation","title":"Authorship, Constitutive Luck, and Neural Causation","timebox":"28:48–86:48","score":{"pro":77,"con":90},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-neural-contingency-nonauthorship","time":"28:48","role":"Supporting reply","words":"A minute synaptic condition could redirect a consequential choice and an entire life, revealing dependence on unauthored physical causes rather than autonomous authorship.","score":71,"critique":"Strongest feature: The synaptic counterfactual vividly illustrates how microscopic physical contingency could redirect consequential choices, making constitutive dependence concrete and supporting the broader challenge to autonomous self-origination. Principal limitation: Physical implementation and causal sensitivity do not themselves establish absence of higher-level control, so the marionette inference bypasses the compatibilist claim that stable reasons-responsive capacities can constitute agency. Live burden: Harris must demonstrate why neural dependence defeats macro-level ownership rather than merely describing its implementation, and must justify extending a possible microscopic difference into a pervasive conclusion about nonauthorship. Locked score: The mixed band reflects a recoverable, relevant, conditionally framed challenge with useful illustrative force, substantially limited by incomplete target contact, restricted evidentiary support, unstable scope, and insufficient engagement with the strongest compatibilist interpretation.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-aln-bounded-self-authorship","time":"60:50","role":"Major direct reply","words":"People can author themselves within limits using unchosen materials and accept responsibility even while luck substantially influences their development and failures.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The limited do-it-yourself metaphor answers absolute nonauthorship without denying found materials, developmental constraint, or substantial luck, thereby presenting self-authorship as bounded construction rather than impossible creation from nothing. Principal limitation: The metaphor clarifies the intended level of agency but does not fully specify when working with inherited materials becomes attributable authorship or how much opportunity and competence are necessary. Live burden: Dennett must operationalize bounded self-construction sufficiently to distinguish genuine revision from causal unfolding, while calibrating responsibility where luck restricts reflective access, alternatives, or developmental resources. Locked score: The very strong band reflects comprehensive responsiveness, explicit limitations, candid accommodation of luck, and stable conceptual framing, with a residual evidentiary and threshold-setting gap preventing the metaphor from independently completing the authorship argument.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-earlier-choice-authorship-regress","time":"58:17","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Tracing an anomalous lapse to an earlier avoidable choice only relocates the problem, because every prior choice remains another causally produced, unauthored event.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The regress reply follows the compatibilist retreat to earlier avoidable choices and consistently reapplies the authorship challenge, preventing temporal relocation from masquerading as an explanation of ultimate origination. Principal limitation: Showing that every earlier choice has antecedent causes does not establish that none can ground bounded responsibility unless ultimate self-authorship has already been accepted as the governing standard. Live burden: Harris must defend that demanding standard without circularity and explain why temporally extended patterns of reflection, foresight, and self-correction cannot support attribution despite causal dependence. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct target contact, clear structure, relevant continuation of the anomalous-lapse exchange, and disciplined causal consistency, moderated by an implicit criterion of authorship and incomplete engagement with the compatibilist alternative.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-aln-macro-authorship","time":"83:07","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Conscious authorship operates through temporally extended high-level control, not awareness or command of every neural microprocess that implements skilled action.","score":92,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move supplies a developed levels-of-control answer through Mozart, tennis, and improvisation, explaining how temporally extended agents can own skilled processes without consciously supervising every neural implementation detail. Principal limitation: Concrete examples make macro-control plausible but do not fully demonstrate that every relevant causal initiation, especially predicted or anomalous action, belongs to the same integrated agent-level process. Live burden: Dennett must specify integration criteria that distinguish genuine extended authorship from post hoc identification with unconscious production, including cases involving manipulation, pathology, or disrupted reasons-responsiveness. Locked score: The very strong band reflects outstanding coherence, evidentiary variety, explicit qualification, charitable target engagement, and comprehensive responsiveness, with only a bounded generalization gap between familiar skilled agency and contested volitional cases.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"alternative-possibilities-randomness","title":"Alternative Possibilities, Replay, and Randomness","timebox":"35:31–93:16","score":{"pro":76,"con":86},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-small-variation-consequential-action","time":"35:31","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Tiny neural differences may separate life-changing actions even outside evenly balanced decisions, so microscopic variation cannot be confined to inconsequential toss-ups.","score":74,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move carefully contests the confinement of microscopic variation to evenly balanced decisions, using qualified language and distinguishing decisions from actions while preserving a consequential causal-luck concern. Principal limitation: Even consequential bit-level sensitivity does not answer why stable competence remains responsibility-relevant, because outcome sensitivity and possession of a reliable action-guiding capacity can coexist within one explanatory framework. Live burden: Harris must connect microscopic divergence to diminished ownership or responsibility without assuming that every implementation-level contingency overrides higher-level deliberation, character, and responsiveness across relevantly similar situations. Locked score: The mixed band reflects precise qualification, coherent possibility reasoning, and a focused counterexample, moderated by indirect responsiveness, limited independent evidence, and failure to displace the competence criterion the example was positioned against.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-apr-competence-alternatives","time":"87:27","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Doing otherwise means possessing competence across a real action range, not producing divergence in an atom-for-atom replay of the identical universe.","score":86,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply clearly distinguishes exact physical replay from robust competence across relevant situations, preserving an action-guiding sense of alternative possibility while directly addressing several opposing moves simultaneously. Principal limitation: Declaring atom-for-atom replay irrelevant is stronger than the brief defense establishes, because Harris's first-person challenge specifically treats identical-circumstances divergence as conceptually important rather than practically convenient. Live burden: Dennett must justify the relevant-similarity standard and explain why counterfactual competence captures deliberative openness without equivocating between categorical ability and variation across altered circumstances. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional responsiveness, stable terminology, coherent reframing, and substantial conceptual warrant, moderated by categorical dismissal of the rival criterion and incomplete explanation of which circumstantial differences preserve genuine alternatives.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-randomness-prediction-no-ownership","time":"90:38","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Randomization supplies no upstream ownership, while sufficiently early neural prediction would challenge conscious authorship by portraying awareness as an outcome of prior causal processing.","score":78,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply separates practical variation from ownership and introduces advance neural prediction as a direct test of conscious initiation, thereby contacting both competence-based alternatives and temporally extended macro-authorship. Principal limitation: The hypothetical experiment remains underspecified regarding accuracy, timing, action complexity, and causal interpretation, while prediction could track the agent's own extended process rather than reveal an alien cause. Live burden: Harris must state what predictive evidence would discriminate nonauthorship from successful modeling of integrated agency and avoid moving from challenged phenomenology to a settled metaphysical conclusion. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct and comprehensive responsiveness, a powerful conceptual point about randomness, and careful hypothetical framing, moderated by limited evidentiary warrant, unstable experimental scope, and incomplete engagement with macro-level ownership.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-apr-indeterminacy-irrelevance","time":"92:34","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Quantum indeterminacy makes no morally relevant difference from deterministic chaos because random divergence adds neither authorship, reasons-responsiveness, nor meaningful control.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly compares deterministic chaos with quantum indeterminacy and identifies moral control, rather than metaphysical randomness itself, as the responsibility-relevant criterion for evaluating alternative causal structures. Principal limitation: The categorical equivalence is largely asserted, without systematically examining whether any sophisticated indeterminist model connects probabilistic openness to agent-level selection rather than merely uncontrolled event variation. Live burden: Dennett must show that every morally relevant contribution attributed to indeterminacy is already supplied by deterministic deliberation or else explain why residual outcome openness cannot enhance authorship. Locked score: The very strong band reflects precise distinctions, exceptional target contact, coherent moral focus, and charitable recognition of the rival premise, moderated by abbreviated warrant and insufficient engagement with stronger agent-causal interpretations.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"moral-responsibility-practice","title":"Moral Responsibility, Accountability, and Social Practice","timebox":"39:51–77:54","score":{"pro":82,"con":88},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-neurophysiology-universal-exculpation","time":"39:51","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Complete neurophysiological explanation would make ordinary wrongdoing exculpatory like brain-tumor behavior because the decisive neural condition was not authored by the agent.","score":75,"critique":"Strongest feature: The brain-tumor analogy directly tests whether complete unauthored neural causation can support responsibility, converting the causal-authorship challenge into a concrete and ethically consequential proposed generalization. Principal limitation: The comparison underdescribes differences between pathological compulsion and ordinary reasons-responsive functioning, so explanatory completeness alone does not establish equivalent impairment, control, or grounds for exculpation. Live burden: Harris must identify the responsibility-relevant feature shared by tumors and normal neural processes without overlooking competence, while explaining why causal explanation eliminates rather than calibrates accountability. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct responsiveness, clear universal scope, intelligible causal reasoning, and substantial moral relevance, tempered by an overextended analogy, hypothetical evidentiary foundations, and insufficient discrimination between neurological causation generally and capacity-disrupting pathology specifically.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-mrp-pragmatic-accountability","time":"63:32","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Free will and legal accountability are pragmatic consequentialist institutions calibrated to human capacities, so responsibility need not depend on absolute metaphysical desert.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply transparently reframes responsibility as a calibrated consequentialist institution, directly rejecting absolute desert while preserving accountability through actual capacities, justified reliance, and practical social consequences. Principal limitation: Pragmatic usefulness alone cannot establish that the resulting practice deserves the title moral responsibility, and the excerpt only briefly distinguishes its account from deterrence and rehabilitation. Live burden: Dennett must show that capacity-sensitive accountability remains ethically constrained, proportionate, and responsive to persons rather than becoming mere behavioral management under inherited responsibility vocabulary. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional relevance, explicit philosophical commitments, coherent reframing, and strong responsiveness to the absolute-responsibility challenge, moderated by incomplete normative foundations and limited specification of consequences beyond institutional usefulness.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-practical-regulation-without-authorship","time":"74:04","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Punishment and stable expectations can regulate behavior, but fuller causal understanding would reveal wrongdoing and reactions as unchosen brain states rather than genuine self-authorship.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply expressly concedes deterrence and institutional regulation, then cleanly separates their practical justification from the disputed ethical and psychological claim of genuine authorship under complete causal understanding. Principal limitation: Its forecast that fuller explanation would reveal all wrongdoing and responsive reactions as unchosen overstates what causal description alone establishes about temporally extended agency and reflective control. Live burden: Harris must explain why acknowledging implementation and causal history negates bounded authorship rather than revising retribution, while preserving the conceded legitimacy of proportionate behavioral regulation. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional target contact, strong representational charity, precise conceptual separation, and substantial central-burden support, with a limited reduction for ambitious explanatory extrapolation and insufficient engagement with macro-level ownership.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-mrp-stable-enforcement","time":"69:41","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Causally explained wrongdoing may still warrant predictable penalties because deterrence, public confidence, and cooperative institutions depend on stable, enforceable, proportionate rules.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The institutional argument concretely connects predictable enforcement with deterrence, confidence, rule-respect, and sustained cooperation, directly showing why causal diagnosis need not eliminate every practical consequence for wrongdoing. Principal limitation: Stable rules do not justify categorical resistance to excuses, because impaired control, proportionality, rehabilitation, and error-sensitive exceptions can support rather than undermine institutional predictability and legitimacy. Live burden: Dennett must distinguish warranted consistency from inflexibility and demonstrate how consequentialist enforcement incorporates individual competence without sacrificing transparency, public reliance, or fair notice. Locked score: The very strong band reflects direct responsiveness, diverse practical examples, clear institutional warrant, and carefully bounded claims about enforcement, tempered by insufficient qualification concerning excusing conditions and the ethical limits governing punishment.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":82,"strengths":["Harris clearly separated first-person authorship from third-person competence, keeping the conceptual-preservation burden visible throughout the exchange.","He repeatedly conceded practical capacities, deterrence, and institutional regulation before arguing that none independently establishes ultimate self-authorship.","His determinism-indeterminism dilemma and regress arguments gave the nonauthorship position a coherent structure extending across conceptual, neural, and moral issues.","The final distinction between random variation and ownership directly answered both competence-based alternatives and appeals to neural unpredictability."],"blunders":[{"text":"The neural-contingency examples often moved too quickly from physical dependence to absence of higher-level control, leaving Dennett's strongest macro-authorship account insufficiently answered.","links":[]},{"text":"The brain-tumor analogy treated ordinary causation and pathological impairment as more responsibility-equivalent than the argument demonstrated.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":88,"strengths":["Dennett developed a detailed positive account of graded agency through attention, ordered desire, reason-responsiveness, self-control, and moral education.","His bounded-control replies consistently rejected absolute self-creation while openly accommodating luck, developmental limitation, and diminished responsibility.","The macro-authorship argument used varied skilled-action examples to explain why unconscious neural implementation need not eliminate temporally extended ownership.","His competence-based account of alternatives and pragmatic responsibility framework supplied connected answers across the debate's metaphysical and institutional dimensions."],"blunders":[{"text":"The naturalistic-revision case initially asserted continuity with the real phenomenon without adequately specifying what survived the conceptual revision.","links":[]},{"text":"Several categorical dismissals, especially of exact replay and indeterminacy's moral relevance, were more confident than their abbreviated supporting arguments warranted.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that reasons-responsive capacities justify regulation and evaluation but cannot establish ultimate moral desert unless the agent authors the standards and dispositions producing action.","premises":["Higher-level descriptions can be causally useful and genuinely explanatory while remaining entirely dependent on constitutive conditions the described agent neither selected nor ultimately controlled.","A person's sensitivity to reasons can justify protection, deterrence, rehabilitation, and prospective expectations without establishing backward-looking desert grounded in ultimate self-creation.","If luck excuses diminished competence because relevant capacities were not adequately available, constitutive luck also requires explanation when those capacities are unusually strong or socially rewarded.","Neural prediction challenges authorship only when it reliably tracks complex choices before the agent-level deliberative process begins, not merely before conscious awareness of its conclusion."],"conclusion":"Therefore compatibilist competence can remain practically indispensable while ultimate desert and self-origination remain unsupported by either deterministic causation or indeterministic variation."},"newArguments":[{"title":"The Symmetry of Constitutive Luck","text":"Responsibility practices already reduce blame when injury, coercion, deprivation, or developmental impairment weakens reasons-responsive capacities. Favorable constitutive conditions should be analyzed by the same principle: unusually effective self-control also depends on opportunities, temperament, education, and neural organization the person did not originate. This does not erase practical distinctions between agents. It instead weakens claims that unequal moral desert tracks capacities whose distribution is substantially fortuitous."},{"title":"Two Responsibility Ledgers","text":"Separate a prospective ledger from a desert ledger. The prospective ledger records who can understand reasons, comply with rules, repair harm, and respond to incentives; it supports calibrated accountability. The desert ledger asks whether suffering is deserved simply because the wrongdoer ultimately authored the source of wrongdoing. Causal nonauthorship leaves the first ledger intact while undermining the second. This division preserves governance without allowing institutional usefulness to settle the metaphysical question by definition."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that free will consists in temporally extended, reasons-responsive self-governance sufficient for reciprocal accountability, not exemption from causation or complete authorship of one's origins.","premises":["Control is meaningful when outcomes systematically vary with an agent's reasons, attention, commitments, and corrective capacities rather than with coercion, compulsion, or disabling impairment.","A process can belong to an agent when it is integrated with memory, commitments, learning, and self-correction across time, even though its neural implementation is unconscious.","Consequentialist accountability is legitimate only when it is proportionate, evidence-sensitive, revisable, and calibrated to competence, thereby excluding indiscriminate punishment and absolute desert.","The relevant ability to do otherwise is reliable performance across nearby circumstances where reasons or opportunities vary, because deliberation evaluates capacities rather than impossible universal replays."],"conclusion":"Therefore causally embedded agents can possess a limited but genuine freedom that supports proportionate accountability whenever integrated capacities allow understanding, deliberation, correction, and dependable response to reasons."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Attributive Integration Test","text":"Authorship should be tested by integration rather than conscious micro-initiation. An action is attributable when its generating process is connected to the person's memories, standing commitments, learned skills, evaluative dispositions, and capacity for later correction. Prediction before awareness does not defeat authorship if the predictor reads that integrated process. By contrast, coercion, seizure, manipulation, or dissociation can break the relevant connections. This test makes macro-authorship discriminating rather than merely metaphorical."},{"title":"Reciprocal Normative Standing","text":"Responsibility is not only a technique for changing behavior; it is also a reciprocal practice among agents capable of offering reasons, recognizing claims, apologizing, making commitments, and repairing harm. Those capacities create a form of normative standing even when nobody authored their original endowment. Institutions should scale demands to actual competence and opportunity. This relational basis explains why accountability can respect persons rather than treating them simply as mechanisms to be managed."}]}},"topicCategory":"free-will-determinism","year":2018};
